Blood pressure is regulated by the interplay between three factors: cardiac output, peripheral resistance, and blood viscosity. Cardiac output (CO) is the amount of blood pumped by the heart per minute, while peripheral resistance (PR) is the resistance of blood vessels to blood flow. Blood viscosity (BV) is the thickness and stickiness of the blood.
According to the Poiseuille's Law, blood pressure (BP) is directly proportional to CO and PR, and inversely proportional to BV. That is,
\begin{equation}
BP = \frac{CO \times PR}{BV}
\end{equation}
An increase in any of these factors can impact blood pressure. For example, an increase in CO, such as during exercise or stress, can increase blood pressure. Similarly, an increase in PR, such as due to narrowing of blood vessels or constriction of smooth muscles in the walls of blood vessels, can also increase blood pressure. On the other hand, a decrease in BV, such as due to dehydration or anemia, can reduce blood pressure.
The kidneys play a crucial role in regulating blood pressure by controlling fluid levels in the body and producing hormones that influence blood vessel constriction and relaxation. The kidneys regulate fluid levels in the body by adjusting the amount of water and electrolytes that are excreted in the urine. This is done by adjusting the permeability of the tubules in the nephrons, the functional units of the kidneys, to water and electrolytes. When blood volume is too low, the kidneys conserve water and electrolytes, which increases blood volume and blood pressure. Conversely, when blood volume is too high, the kidneys excrete excess water and electrolytes, which reduces blood volume and blood pressure.
The kidneys also produce hormones that influence blood vessel constriction and relaxation. One such hormone is renin, which is produced by special cells in the kidneys called juxtaglomerular cells. Renin is released in response to low blood pressure or low blood volume, and it activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS). The RAAS is a complex system that ultimately results in the production of angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor that increases peripheral resistance and blood pressure.
Another hormone produced by the kidneys is erythropoietin (EPO), which stimulates the production of red blood cells. An increase in red blood cells can increase blood viscosity, which can increase blood pressure. However, EPO also has vasodilatory effects, which can counteract the vasoconstrictive effects of angiotensin II.
In summary, blood pressure is regulated by the interplay between cardiac output, peripheral resistance, and blood viscosity. An increase in any of these factors can increase blood pressure, while a decrease can reduce blood pressure. The kidneys play a crucial role in regulating blood pressure by controlling fluid levels in the body and producing hormones that influence blood vessel constriction and relaxation.

