To handle more complex sentences with multiple verbs and irregular verbs, you can modify the JavaScript code to use a natural language processing (NLP) library like Natural or Compromise. These libraries can help you parse the sentence and identify the verbs, their tense, and their irregular forms.
Here's an example of how you can modify the code to use the Natural library:
```javascript
const natural = require('natural');
const verbInflector = new natural.Conjugation();
function toPastTense(sentence) {
  const tokens = new natural.WordTokenizer().tokenize(sentence);
  const pastTokens = tokens.map(token => {
    if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'is') > 0.9) {
      return 'was';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'are') > 0.9) {
      return 'were';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'have') > 0.9) {
      return 'had';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'do') > 0.9) {
      return 'did';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'be') > 0.9) {
      return 'been';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'go') > 0.9) {
      return 'went';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'see') > 0.9) {
      return 'saw';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'come') > 0.9) {
      return 'came';
    } else if (natural.JaroWinklerDistance(token, 'make') > 0.9) {
      return 'made';
    } else {
      const tag = new natural.BrillPOSTagger().tag([token])[0][1];
      if (tag.startsWith('VB')) {
        return verbInflector.inflect(token, 'past');
      } else {
        return token;
      }
    }
  });
  return pastTokens.join(' ');
}
```
In this modified code, we first tokenize the sentence using the `WordTokenizer` from the Natural library. We then iterate over each token and check if it matches any of the common irregular verbs using the `JaroWinklerDistance` function, which measures the similarity between two strings. If the token matches an irregular verb, we return its past tense form.
If the token is not an irregular verb, we use the `BrillPOSTagger` from the Natural library to tag the token with its part of speech. If the token is a verb, we use the `Conjugation` module from the Natural library to inflect it to its past tense form. If the token is not a verb, we simply return it.
With this modified code, you can handle more complex sentences with multiple verbs and irregular verbs.

